.TH PAC1954 4 2026-11-16 "creature-cm5-sensors" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
pac1954 \- Microchip PAC1954 power monitor hwmon driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modprobe pac1954
.PP
.B sensors pac1954\-*
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pac1954
is a Linux hwmon driver for the Microchip PAC1954 four\-channel power
monitor.
For each channel it reports the bus voltage, the current through the shunt
resistor, and the power.
It is packaged for the Creature CM5 Carrier board, which has a PAC1954 at
address 0x10 and an MCP9808 temperature sensor at 0x18, both on I2C1
(GPIO2/3).
The MCP9808 uses the in\-kernel
.B jc42
driver.
.PP
The driver is built with DKMS, so a new kernel gets a new build of the
module automatically, as long as its headers are installed (the package
depends on
.BR linux\-headers\-rpi\-2712 ).
.PP
When it starts, the driver checks the product and manufacturer IDs and then
configures the chip: 1024 samples per second with adaptive accumulation, all
channels on, unipolar ranges, alerts off, and the SLOW/ALERT1 pin set to
ALERT.
The chip's power\-on default for that pin is a SLOW input, and the carrier
board pulls it high, which would hold the chip at 8 samples per second.
.SH SYSFS ATTRIBUTES
Attributes are in
.IR /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon N /
where
.I name
is
.BR pac1954 .
Channels are numbered 1 to 4, matching the datasheet's CH1 to CH4.
.TP
.BR in N _input ", " in N _average
Bus voltage in millivolts: the latest sample, and the chip's rolling average
of 8 samples.
Range 0 to 32 V.
.TP
.BR curr N _input ", " curr N _average
Current in milliamperes, the sense voltage divided by the channel's shunt
resistance.
The sense range is 0 to 100 mV, so 25 A with a 4 m\(*W shunt.
Present only when the channel has a shunt value.
.TP
.BR power N _input
Power in microwatts, from the chip's VBUS \(mu VSENSE product.
Present only when the channel has a shunt value.
.TP
.BR in N _label ", " curr N _label ", " power N _label
The channel's label from the device tree.
.PP
Readings are cached for 100 ms.
Each update sends REFRESH_V, which latches new results without clearing the
chip's accumulators, and then reads all channels in one transfer.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The device is declared in the device tree.
On the Creature CM5 Carrier board the package's overlay,
.BR creature\-cm5\-sensors ,
does this.
Each channel is a child node with a
.B reg
(1 to 4), a
.B shunt\-resistor\-micro\-ohms
and an optional
.BR label :
.PP
.RS
.nf
power\-monitor@10 {
    compatible = "microchip,pac1954";
    reg = <0x10>;
    #address\-cells = <1>;
    #size\-cells = <0>;
    channel@1 {
        reg = <1>;
        shunt\-resistor\-micro\-ohms = <4000>;
        label = "Board In";
    };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
Channels without a node are hidden.
If there are no channel nodes at all, all four channels are shown with
voltage only.
.PP
The Creature CM5 Carrier channels are:
.TP
.B 1 "Board In"
SYS_24V, the total board input after the ideal\-diode ORing.
.TP
.B 2 "CM5 5V"
The 5.1 V rail into the CM5.
.TP
.B 3 Character
USB\-C character power.
.TP
.B 4 PoE
The AG5810 output only, which is PoE's share of the input.
.PP
All four shunts are 4 m\(*W.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/src/pac1954\-*/
Driver source, which DKMS builds for each kernel.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/creature\-cm5\-sensors/creature\-cm5\-sensors.dtbo
The board overlay, copied to
.I /boot/firmware/overlays/
when the package is installed or upgraded.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/config.txt
The package adds a block between
.B "# BEGIN creature\-cm5\-sensors"
and
.B "# END creature\-cm5\-sensors"
that enables the overlay, and removes it when the package is removed.
If
.B dtoverlay=creature\-cm5\-sensors
is already present elsewhere in the file, no block is added.
At install time the overlay is also applied to the running system with
.BR dtoverlay (1),
so no reboot is needed.
.TP
.I /etc/sensors.d/creature\-cm5\-sensors.conf
.BR sensors.conf (5)
labels, such as "Board" for the MCP9808.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60\-creature\-cm5\-sensors.rules
Sets the MCP9808 temperature limits (min \-20 \(deC, max 70 \(deC,
critical 85 \(deC) when the jc42 device appears.
The chip's limit registers power up at 0 \(deC, which reads as a permanent
alarm.
To change the limits, copy this file to
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/
under the same name and edit it.
.SH NOTES
At light load the current readings have a fixed offset of a few tens of
milliamperes, which is about 0.1% of full scale.
.PP
While the driver is bound,
.BR i2cget (8)
and
.BR i2ctransfer (8)
need
.B \-f
to reach address 0x10.
Only read with them: a REFRESH from user space disturbs the driver's
readings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sensors (1),
.BR sensors.conf (5),
.BR dkms (8),
.BR dtoverlay (1)
